Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables and columns (with their potential usage)

• payment  
  – payment_id: unique transaction identifier (usually not needed for aggregation)  
  – customer_id: links each payment to a customer (join key, grouping key)  
  – amount: dollar value of each payment (what we sum / compare)  
  – payment_date: timestamp that lets us extract the year‑month for “monthly” totals  

• customer  
  – customer_id: primary key to join with payment.customer_id  
  – first_name / last_name: displayable customer name in the final answer  

Typical conditions / filters we will need

• GROUP BY customer_id to find total payment per customer  
• ORDER BY total DESC LIMIT 10 to isolate the 10 biggest spenders  
• strftime('%Y-%m', payment_date) (SQLite) to convert each payment into a “YYYY‑MM” bucket  
• For the month‑over‑month step we will later join a month’s total to the immediately following month’s total (e.g. using a window lead or a self‑join on month = prev_month + 1)

Exploratory SQL queries
(The following ten queries progress from simple inspection to pieces that will be reused in the final solution. Each query is annotated and capped with LIMIT 20.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at raw payment rows
SELECT *
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct list of customer IDs that ever made a payment
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
FROM "payment"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Total amount each customer has paid (unsorted sample)
SELECT "customer_id",
       ROUND(SUM("amount"), 2) AS "total_paid"
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. The 10 highest‑paying customers (overall total)
SELECT "customer_id",
       ROUND(SUM("amount"), 2) AS "total_paid"
FROM "payment"
GROUP BY "customer_id"
ORDER BY "total_paid" DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join the 10 highest‑paying customers to their names
SELECT c."customer_id",
       c."first_name" || ' ' || c."last_name" AS "customer_name",
       ROUND(SUM(p."amount"), 2) AS "total_paid"
FROM   "payment" AS p
JOIN   "customer" AS c ON c."customer_id" = p."customer_id"
GROUP  BY c."customer_id"
ORDER  BY "total_paid" DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 6. Monthly payment totals for one example customer (ID = 1)
SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', "payment_date") AS "month",
       ROUND(SUM("amount"), 2)           AS "monthly_total"
FROM   "payment"
WHERE  "customer_id" = 1
GROUP  BY "month"
ORDER  BY "month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Monthly totals for ALL customers (needed for MoM calc later)
SELECT "customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', "payment_date") AS "month",
       ROUND(SUM("amount"), 2)           AS "monthly_total"
FROM   "payment"
GROUP  BY "customer_id", "month"
ORDER  BY "customer_id", "month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as 7 but restricted to the top‑10 customers found in query 4
WITH top10 AS (
    SELECT "customer_id"
    FROM   "payment"
    GROUP  BY "customer_id"
    ORDER  BY SUM("amount") DESC
    LIMIT 10
)
SELECT p."customer_id",
       strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "month",
       ROUND(SUM(p."amount"), 2)           AS "monthly_total"
FROM   "payment" AS p
JOIN   top10     ON top10."customer_id" = p."customer_id"
GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "month"
ORDER  BY p."customer_id", "month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Add a LEAD() window to compute the difference to the next month
WITH monthly AS (
    SELECT p."customer_id",
           strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "month",
           ROUND(SUM(p."amount"), 2)           AS "monthly_total"
    FROM   "payment" AS p
    GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "month"
)
SELECT "customer_id",
       "month",
       "monthly_total",
       LEAD("monthly_total") OVER (
           PARTITION BY "customer_id"
           ORDER BY "month"
       )                                     AS "next_month_total",
       ROUND(
           ABS("monthly_total" - LEAD("monthly_total") OVER (
               PARTITION BY "customer_id" ORDER BY "month"
           )), 2)                            AS "mom_diff"
FROM   monthly
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Combine top‑10 filter with MoM difference and pick the maximum
WITH top10 AS (
    SELECT "customer_id"
    FROM   "payment"
    GROUP  BY "customer_id"
    ORDER  BY SUM("amount") DESC
    LIMIT 10
),
monthly AS (
    SELECT p."customer_id",
           strftime('%Y-%m', p."payment_date") AS "month",
           ROUND(SUM(p."amount"), 2)           AS "monthly_total"
    FROM   "payment" AS p
    WHERE  p."customer_id" IN (SELECT "customer_id" FROM top10)
    GROUP  BY p."customer_id", "month"
),
diffs AS (
    SELECT "customer_id",
           "month",
           LEAD("monthly_total") OVER (
               PARTITION BY "customer_id"
               ORDER BY "month"
           ) - "monthly_total" AS "month_to_next"
    FROM   monthly
)
SELECT d."customer_id",
       d."month"                        AS "current_month",
       ROUND(ABS(d."month_to_next"), 2) AS "abs_mom_difference"
FROM   diffs AS d
WHERE  d."month_to_next" IS NOT NULL
ORDER  BY ABS(d."month_to_next") DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries explore all the columns, filters, and techniques required to answer: for which top‑10 customer and in which month was the largest month‑over‑month payment change, and what was that amount (rounded to two decimals).
